{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0124c471KeSxvpBQS9uCrMcq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft reference gap analysis\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** ready. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n4\t\n5\t## Overall assessment\n6\tThe draft is well supported on adaptive data analysis (Dwork, Blum, Hardt and others), post-selection inference (Berk, Andrews, Efron) and the scoring-rule and Wilson baselines. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`, so they were ignored here. Three places are under-cited.\n7\t\n8\t## Missing literatures\n9\t- Item response theory and latent-difficulty models, including their use in LLM evaluation.\n10\t- Selection bias and overfitting in ML model selection and hyperparameter search.\n11\t- Automatic prompt optimization beyond DSPy and MIPRO.\n12\t\n13\t## Rewrite targets\n14\t\n15\t1. **method-irt-model-background** (method, high, background)\n16\t   - Anchor: \"each logged outcome follows y_{qk} ~ Bernoulli(sigma(a_k - b_q))\".\n17\t   - The reporter is called EB-IRT, but no IRT, Rasch or hierarchical-IRT work is cited, and the empirical-Bayes and Laplace fitting is not tied to prior work.\n18\t   - Search topics: Rasch and IRT foundations; hierarchical Bayesian IRT with empirical-Bayes or Laplace fitting; IRT for LLM benchmark evaluation.\n19\t\n20\t2. **intro-selection-bias-ml-model-selection** (introduction, high, positioning)\n21\t   - Anchor: \"It is therefore a biased estimate of that candidate's accuracy on new questions, and the usual interval around it ignores the selection.\"\n22\t   - The central motivating claim has no support from the ML literature on model-selection bias. Related work covers statistics and holdout reuse, plus one image-classification study.\n23\t   - Search topics: overfitting in model selection; bias of cross-validation error when selecting among many models; meta-analyses of benchmark overfitting.\n24\t\n25\t3. **related-work-prompt-optimizers** (related work, medium, comparator)\n26\t   - Anchor: \"DSPy and MIPRO automate prompt and pipeline optimization\".\n27\t   - The claim that no existing prompt-search work gives selection-aware uncertainty rests on only three cited works.\n28\t   - Search topics: LLM-driven automatic prompt optimization; evaluation overfitting in prompt optimization.\n29\t\n30\t## Not flagged\n31\t- Adaptive data analysis, post-selection inference and scoring rules are already adequately cited.\n32\t- The ignorability and degenerate-world discussion is a design argument and does not need extra citations.\n33\t- Model and benchmark citations (Qwen2.5, MMLU-Pro) are present.\n34\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"656c76ab-65c2-4296-9c0d-84c67e8d12ff","uuid":"1ce17435-8a4e-4093-b8ca-4f2c4fba430c","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:46:14.281Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.md","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Overall assessment\nThe draft is well supported on adaptive data analysis (Dwork, Blum, Hardt and others), post-selection inference (Berk, Andrews, Efron) and the scoring-rule and Wilson baselines. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`, so they were ignored here. Three places are under-cited.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Item response theory and latent-difficulty models, including their use in LLM evaluation.\n- Selection bias and overfitting in ML model selection and hyperparameter search.\n- Automatic prompt optimization beyond DSPy and MIPRO.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n1. **method-irt-model-background** (method, high, background)\n   - Anchor: \"each logged outcome follows y_{qk} ~ Bernoulli(sigma(a_k - b_q))\".\n   - The reporter is called EB-IRT, but no IRT, Rasch or hierarchical-IRT work is cited, and the empirical-Bayes and Laplace fitting is not tied to prior work.\n   - Search topics: Rasch and IRT foundations; hierarchical Bayesian IRT with empirical-Bayes or Laplace fitting; IRT for LLM benchmark evaluation.\n\n2. **intro-selection-bias-ml-model-selection** (introduction, high, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"It is therefore a biased estimate of that candidate's accuracy on new questions, and the usual interval around it ignores the selection.\"\n   - The central motivating claim has no support from the ML literature on model-selection bias. Related work covers statistics and holdout reuse, plus one image-classification study.\n   - Search topics: overfitting in model selection; bias of cross-validation error when selecting among many models; meta-analyses of benchmark overfitting.\n\n3. **related-work-prompt-optimizers** (related work, medium, comparator)\n   - Anchor: \"DSPy and MIPRO automate prompt and pipeline optimization\".\n   - The claim that no existing prompt-search work gives selection-aware uncertainty rests on only three cited works.\n   - Search topics: LLM-driven automatic prompt optimization; evaluation overfitting in prompt optimization.\n\n## Not flagged\n- Adaptive data analysis, post-selection inference and scoring rules are already adequately cited.\n- The ignorability and degenerate-world discussion is a design argument and does not need extra citations.\n- Model and benchmark citations (Qwen2.5, MMLU-Pro) are present.\n","numLines":34,"startLine":1,"totalLines":34}}}
