nd that the usual interval ignores selection is stated without support from the ML model-selection and hyperparameter-search literature, where this is a well-known problem. Related work covers only statistical and holdout-reuse literature plus one image-classification study, so positioning against ML selection bias is thin.\",\n29\t      \"anchor_text\": \"It is therefore a biased estimate of that candidate's accuracy on new questions, and the usual interval around it ignores the selection.\",\n30\t      \"search_topics\": [\n31\t        \"over-fitting in model selection and selection bias in performance evaluation\",\n32\t        \"bias in cross-validation error estimates when selecting among many models\",\n33\t        \"meta-analysis of benchmark overfitting from repeated test-set reuse\"\n34\t      ]\n35\t    },\n36\t    {\n37\t      \"target_id\": \"related-work-prompt-optimizers\",\n38\t      \"section\": \"related work\",\n39\t      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n40\t      \"citation_intent\": \"comparator\",\n41\t      \"reason\": \"The paragraph on prompt search names only DSPy, MIPRO and a best-arm formulation as the optimizers whose reported scores are at risk. Well-known LLM-driven prompt optimizers are not cited, so the claim that none of these works supplies a post-search score with selection-aware uncertainty rests on a narrow sample.\",\n42\t      \"anchor_text\": \"DSPy and MIPRO automate prompt and pipeline optimization\",\n43\t      \"search_topics\": [\n44\t        \"automatic prompt optimization with large language models\",\n45\t        \"evaluation overfitting in prompt optimization on small validation sets\"\n46\t      ]\n47\t    }\n48\t  ],\n49\t  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n50\t}\n51\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"656c76ab-65c2-4296-9c0d-84c67e8d12ff","uuid":"dcc62536-0435-48a7-8e65-ec5ef3545e40","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:46:14.603Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.json","